================================================================================
=== REASONING ===
================================================================================

Why this slice's design preserves ADR-015, P1-P10, and the non-AI
baseline:

1. ADR-015 §I1 default-off: registry entry has DefaultOn: false.
   Confirmed via go test ./internal/ai/features/... — the
   off-mode coverage assertion passes (zero rendered surfaces in
   the off-mode invariant test).

2. ADR-015 §I3 baseline intact: SmartChargePage continues to render
   the canonical schedule settings form, the Charge Settings form,
   the optimized-schedule result card, the RateTimeline, and the
   Plans list. The new AI surface is a sibling <FadeIn> panel ABOVE
   the Settings Section; it does NOT wrap, replace, hide, or
   short-circuit any baseline element. The deterministic
   computeSchedule() is untouched in semantics — only refactored out
   of Optimize() into a pure function so the AI tool path can reuse
   it without bypassing the canonical validation. The off-mode Go
   test (TestSmartChargeScheduleAIOffManualScheduleWorks in
   internal/api/ai_smart_charge_schedule_handler_test.go) proves the
   /api/v1/charge-planner/optimize baseline still returns its
   canonical optimizeResponse envelope when ai_mode='off' — i.e. the
   request never reaches the AI handler at all.

3. ADR-015 §I3 (refactor safety): computeSchedule extraction
   preserves the original Optimize handler's contract. The new
   Optimize handler reads SOC, calls computeSchedule for the
   schedule + savings + rates, persists the plan, increments the
   chargePlanCreatedTotal counter, and writes the response — exact
   same observable behaviour as before the refactor. Existing
   ChargePlannerHandler.Optimize tests in the api package continue
   to pass (go test ./internal/api/ -count=1 ok).

4. ADR-015 §I4 zero egress in off mode: the AI route is mounted via
   `r.Post("/charging/schedule/draft", g.Wrap("smart-charge-
   schedule-suggestion", h.ServeHTTP))` inside mountAIRoutes. The
   guard short-circuits to 404 + "not_found" body whenever
   getAIMode(ctx)='off' OR AIFeatureEnabled('smart-charge-schedule-
   suggestion')==false, BEFORE any handler or provider code runs.
   No provider call, no tool call, no LLM token consumption. The
   off-mode test asserts both the 404 status and the "not_found"
   body (so we don't accidentally leak a more descriptive error
   that would hint at the route's existence).

5. ADR-015 §I5 hidden UI: AISmartChargeScheduleSuggestion is wrapped
   by withAiFeature('smart-charge-schedule-suggestion', …). The HOC
   reads useSettings().settings; returns null whenever ai_mode='off'
   OR ai_features['smart-charge-schedule-suggestion']==false.
   TestSmartChargeScheduleAIOffManualScheduleWorks (the React test)
   asserts (a) the test ID is absent from the DOM in off mode, (b)
   the container is empty, AND (c) the positive control with
   ai_mode='cloud' + toggle=true DOES render and DOES carry the
   expected test ID. The positive control matters — without it the
   off-mode assertions would be trivially true if a typo
   permanently hid the section.

6. ADR-015 §I6 404 routes: the guarded route returns 404 with body
   "not_found\n" (no internal error revealing the route's purpose)
   when ai_mode='off'. This is the same body produced by
   guard.Wrap in every other off-mode-guarded route. The slice
   registry route metadata Backend: ["POST /api/v1/ai/charging/
   schedule/draft"] mirrors the actual mount path so tools/aivet's
   coverage check passes.

7. ADR-015 §I7 per-feature gate: the registry entry's NeedsTools=true
   + NeedsStream=true + Tier=C is the per-feature contract. The
   toggle is a boolean in settings.ai_features['smart-charge-
   schedule-suggestion']. Even with ai_mode='cloud', a toggle=false
   hides the UI and 404s the route (verified by
   TestSmartChargeScheduleAIOffManualScheduleWorks case 2: "renders
   nothing when ai_mode is non-off but the toggle is false"). This
   defeats the shortcut "off because nothing is enabled" — both
   halves of the gate are independently load-bearing.

8. ADR-015 §I10 type-system enforcement: every JSON boundary is a
   typed Go struct or a typed TypeScript interface. The AI handler
   uses DisallowUnknownFields() on its body decoder; tool arguments
   are validated by tools.ToolSchema. No `any`, no
   `map[string]interface{}` at boundary, no `interface{}` in the
   tool return paths. The AIChargeScheduleComputer adapter declares
   `var _ tools.ChargeScheduleComputer = (*AIChargeScheduleComputer)(nil)`
   as a compile-time interface check (passes; not asserted at
   runtime).

9. P1 provider abstraction: the strategy file imports only from
   internal/ai/strategy + internal/ai/redact + internal/ai/features;
   it does NOT import internal/ai/adapters/openai or
   internal/ai/adapters/local. The provider dispatch is by
   strategy.Dispatcher injected at construction time in router.go.

10. P2 strategy registration: the strategy.go file exports
    FeatureID = "smart-charge-schedule-suggestion" and a Strategy
    struct that satisfies strategy.Strategy. Registered in the
    dispatcher's strategyRegistry map in router.go at the slice's
    construction site.

11. P3 system prompt determinism: SystemPrompt is a const string
    pinned by strategy_test.go (TestSystemPrompt_ContainsLoadBearing
    Strings). The 9 load-bearing substrings ("TeslaSync smart-
    charge-schedule agent", "NEVER save anything", "ALWAYS follow
    this tool sequence", "draft_charge_schedule",
    "validate_charge_schedule", "Do NOT invent facts", "Refuse
    politely", "never invent alternate cost numbers", "never
    fabricate rate-plan tiers", "never quote precise street
    addresses") would each fail the test if removed.

12. P4 dispatcher loop: the AI handler invokes the dispatcher, which
    drives the tool-call loop (planner asks for tools, executes
    them, feeds results back). The dispatcher is the SAME one used
    by every C-tier and D-tier slice; no per-slice loop variants.

13. P5 decorator chain: providers are wrapped in fixed order at
    construction time in router.go (NewProviderFromConfig and the
    decorator.Wrap chain that follows): redaction (F8) -> rate
    limit (F9) -> cost cap (F9) -> audit (F3) -> trace (F0). The
    chain is per-feature so the smart-charge feature picks up its
    own redaction policy + its own rate-limit bucket. Verified by
    inspection — no per-slice override path exists.

14. P6 frontend guard primitives: AISmartChargeScheduleSuggestion
    uses withAiFeature (the canonical HOC), which internally
    consumes useAiEnabled('smart-charge-schedule-suggestion'). No
    bespoke gate logic in the slice's component.

15. P7 retrieval (F7): this slice does NOT use retrieval. NeedsRAG
    is false in the registry. The strategy file does not import
    rag or embeddings packages. The future-W1 slice may wire a
    retrieval primer over historical charge_session rows but it is
    explicitly out of scope here.

16. P8 eval goldens (F6): goldens.yaml contains 3 deterministic
    cases:
      - happy_path_off_peak_window: agent calls draft+validate, then
        narrates the proposed midnight-6am window with savings_vs_now.
      - no_savings_already_off_peak: agent calls draft, sees savings=0,
        explains current schedule is already optimal.
      - refusal_other_vehicle: user asks the agent to plan for a
        different vehicle; agent refuses without calling any tools.
    Each golden has must_call_tools (or must_not_call_tools for
    refusal), answer_must_contain, and answer_must_not_contain
    assertions. The harness runs them via canned mock-provider
    replies (3 YAML files in canned/), so they're 100% deterministic.

17. P9 feature registry (F0): registry entry has all 5 RouteSet
    fields populated explicitly. Backend includes the exact route
    string "POST /api/v1/ai/charging/schedule/draft". JobNames and
    PushKinds are EMPTY ARRAYS (not omitted), so the W2 final gate
    can prove absence of these surfaces in off mode without needing
    to special-case missing fields.

18. P10 baseline coexistence: ChargeScheduleAdvisor selection is by
    ai_mode + smart-charge-schedule-suggestion toggle; manual
    settings remain baseline. The baseline POST /api/v1/charge-
    planner/optimize handler is preserved (only internally refactored
    to extract computeSchedule). The AI route /api/v1/ai/charging/
    schedule/draft is a NEW guarded route that proposes a schedule
    but never saves; users must click the baseline Schedule button
    (which calls /api/v1/charge-planner/optimize via useApplySchedule)
    to commit anything. The off-mode test
    TestSmartChargeScheduleAIOffManualScheduleWorks is the
    load-bearing proof that the baseline is exercised when AI is off.

